# Line endings.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which
# rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. That is harmless for everything here except
# the one file that has to run on a Linux host.
#
# scripts/kora-runner.sh is installed on a VPS and executed by systemd. With
# CRLF endings its shebang reads `/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and the kernel looks for
# an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage return. The error is
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory", which points at bash rather than
# at the line endings and sends people looking in the wrong place.
#
# The working tree happened to hold LF when the script was first copied to the
# VPS, so this was not hit. A fresh clone would have hit it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else a Linux host executes or parses verbatim.
*.bash text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf
